Yes: it removes the focus indicator for keyboard users too, and without it they can't see where they are on the page. That's a WCAG failure. Target `:focus-visible` instead; browsers apply it when focus should be shown, typically during keyboard navigation, and not after a mouse click on a button.

Remove the `*:focus` rule and give the focus ring your own style:

```css
:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--focus-ring, #1a5fb4);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

Current browsers already use this logic for their default ring. So if you still see an outline when clicking a button with the mouse, something in your CSS or a component library is styling `:focus` directly; change that rule to `:focus-visible`. Text inputs are the exception by design: they show focus on click too, because you're about to type in them.

Pick a ring color with at least 3:1 contrast against the backgrounds it appears on, and prefer `outline` over `box-shadow`: outlines stay visible in Windows high contrast mode, shadows are removed.

the .env is in /home/kim/api next to index.js and I run node index.js from there. and here's the weird part: console.log(process.env.ALLOWED_HOSTS) in index.js prints the right value!

index.js:
import dotenv from "dotenv";
import express from "express";
import { allowedHosts } from "./config.js";

dotenv.config();
console.log(process.env.ALLOWED_HOSTS);

That output rules out the `.env` path; the problem is ordering. In ES modules, all `import` declarations are resolved and the imported modules are executed before any statement in `index.js` runs. So `config.js` executes first and reads `process.env.ALLOWED_HOSTS`, and only afterwards does `dotenv.config()` fill in the environment. The `console.log` runs later still, which is why it looks fine.

Load dotenv as an import that comes before the one for `config.js`:

```js
import "dotenv/config";
import express from "express";
import { allowedHosts } from "./config.js";
```

Imports are evaluated in order, so `dotenv/config` populates `process.env` before `config.js` runs. On Node 20.6 or newer you can drop dotenv entirely and start with `node --env-file=.env index.js`, which sets the variables before any module loads.

Either way the `split` error should be gone. To make a missing variable fail with a clearer message in the future, you could check it in `config.js` and throw `new Error("ALLOWED_HOSTS is not set")`.

