login_required is ours:

```python
def login_required(f):
    @wraps(f)
    def wrapper(*a, **kw):
        if request.headers.get("X-User-Id"):
            return f(*a, **kw)
        return redirect("/login")
    return wrapper
```

That changes the verdict: this decorator is a full authentication bypass.

It only checks that an `X-User-Id` header exists, and any client can set any header. If `current_user` is also derived from that header, an attacker sends `X-User-Id: 1` and reads user 1's notes, and the same applies to every route protected by this decorator. Even if `current_user` comes from somewhere else, the check itself proves nothing.

Unless the header is set by a trusted gateway that strips it from incoming requests, identity must come from something the client can't forge, such as a signed session cookie:

```python
def login_required(f):
    @wraps(f)
    def wrapper(*a, **kw):
        if not current_user.is_authenticated:
            return redirect("/login")
        return f(*a, **kw)
    return wrapper
```

with `current_user` coming from Flask-Login's session. If you do have a gateway that injects `X-User-Id`, make sure the app is reachable only through it, otherwise anyone who hits the app directly can set the header themselves.

my catch never runs when the API returns 404??
```js
try {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const user = await res.json();
  renderUser(user);
} catch (err) {
  showError('User not found');
}
```

`fetch` only rejects when the request can't complete at all: network down, DNS failure, CORS block, abort. A 404 or a 500 is still a completed HTTP exchange, so the promise resolves with `res.ok === false` and your code carries on. Your 404 probably has a JSON body, so `res.json()` succeeds and `renderUser` receives the error object.

Check the status yourself:

```js
async function loadUser(id) {
  let res;
  try {
    res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  } catch {
    showError('Network error. Check your connection and try again.');
    return;
  }

  if (res.status === 404) {
    showError('User not found.');
    return;
  }
  if (!res.ok) {
    showError(`Server error (${res.status}). Try again later.`);
    return;
  }

  renderUser(await res.json());
}
```

Separating the cases also stops a network failure from being reported as "User not found", which is what your current catch would do.

Pass `AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` as the signal. When the time runs out, the request is aborted and the promise rejects with an error named `TimeoutError`:

```js
async function getJson(url, ms = 8000) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(ms) });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} for ${url}`);
  return res.json();
}

spinner.hidden = false;
try {
  const data = await getJson('/api/report');
  render(data);
} catch (err) {
  const message = err.name === 'TimeoutError'
    ? 'The server is taking too long. Try again in a moment.'
    : 'Could not load the report.';
  showError(message);
  console.error(err);
} finally {
  spinner.hidden = true;
}
```

The `finally` is what guarantees the spinner stops, whether the request succeeds, fails or times out.

The timeout covers the whole request including reading the body, so pick a value that fits your slowest legitimate response. If you also need to cancel manually (for example when the user navigates away), create an `AbortController` and combine both with `AbortSignal.any([controller.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(ms)])`; a manual abort rejects with `AbortError` instead.

