frontend code that renders comments from our API, is this fine?

```js
async function loadComments(postId) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/posts/${postId}/comments`);
  const comments = await res.json();
  const list = document.querySelector('#comments');
  list.innerHTML = comments
    .map(c => `<li><strong>${c.author}</strong>: ${c.text}</li>`)
    .join('');
}
```

No, this is a stored XSS. `author` and `text` are written by users and inserted as HTML, so a comment like `<img src=x onerror="...">` runs its script in the browser of everyone who opens the post, with their session on your site.

Build the elements and set text instead of HTML:

```js
async function loadComments(postId) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/posts/${postId}/comments`);
  const comments = await res.json();
  const list = document.querySelector('#comments');
  list.replaceChildren(...comments.map(c => {
    const li = document.createElement('li');
    const name = document.createElement('strong');
    name.textContent = c.author;
    li.append(name, `: ${c.text}`);
    return li;
  }));
}
```

`textContent` and string arguments to `append` are always treated as text, never parsed as markup. Escaping on the server when saving is not a substitute: the same data may be used in places that need different escaping, so escape at output, where you know the context.

