`deepcopy` (and `pickle`) build the copy without calling `__init__`, so the new object has no `_values` yet. When `copy` checks `hasattr(y, '__setstate__')`, normal lookup fails and Python calls your `__getattr__`, which reads `self._values`; that attribute is missing too, so `__getattr__("_values")` runs, reads `self._values` again, and it never ends.

Make `__getattr__` refuse private and dunder names before touching `_values`:

```python
    def __getattr__(self, name):
        if name.startswith("_"):
            raise AttributeError(name)
        try:
            return self._values[name]
        except KeyError:
            raise AttributeError(name) from None
```

`__getattr__` only runs when normal lookup fails, so this doesn't affect `self._values` on a fully initialized object; it just stops the lookup from recursing on a half-built one. With this change `copy.deepcopy(s).debug` and `pickle.loads(pickle.dumps(s)).debug` both work, and the copy gets its own `_values` dict.

