why does this print 4 4 4 instead of 1 2 3

for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 1000);
}

`var` creates a single `i` for the whole function, not one per iteration. All three callbacks close over that same variable, and by the time they run the loop has finished and `i` is 4.

Change `var` to `let`:

```js
for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 1000);
}
// 1, 2, 3, one per second
```

With `let`, a `for` loop creates a fresh binding of `i` for each iteration, so each callback captures its own value. The delays were already right in your version because `i * 1000` is evaluated immediately; only the callbacks read `i` later.

When a request carries cookies, the browser requires the server to name the exact origin and explicitly allow credentials; `cors()` with no options sends `*`, which is rejected in that mode.

```js
app.use(cors({
  origin: "http://localhost:5173",
  credentials: true,
}));
```

That produces `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:5173` and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, and the browser will send and accept the cookie. For several environments pass an array of allowed origins, ideally built from an environment variable.

Don't "fix" it with `origin: true` (reflect whatever origin asks) combined with `credentials: true` in production: that lets any website make authenticated requests with your users' cookies and read the responses.

If the cookie still doesn't show up afterwards, check its attributes: a cross-site cookie needs `SameSite=None; Secure`, while `localhost:5173` and `localhost:3000` count as the same site, so `SameSite=Lax` works in dev.

