Drop the hand-made Promise. `client.messages.stream()` returns a stream object whose `finalMessage()` already resolves when the message completes and rejects on errors or aborts, so a normal `try`/`await` works:

```ts
import Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";

const client = new Anthropic();
const MODEL = process.env.ANTHROPIC_MODEL!;

async function ask(question: string): Promise<Anthropic.Message> {
  const stream = client.messages.stream({
    model: MODEL,
    max_tokens: 2048,
    messages: [{ role: "user", content: question }],
  });

  stream.on("text", (delta) => process.stdout.write(delta));

  const final = await stream.finalMessage();
  process.stdout.write("\n");
  return final;
}

try {
  const msg = await ask("Explain JavaScript event loop phases briefly.");
  console.log(`stop: ${msg.stop_reason}, output tokens: ${msg.usage.output_tokens}`);
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Anthropic.APIError) console.error(`API error ${err.status}: ${err.message}`);
  else throw err;
}
```

Use the `text` deltas only for display. Anything you store (conversation history, logs, token accounting) should come from the final message, which is complete even if you missed a delta.

If the user cancels, call `stream.abort()`; the pending `finalMessage()` then rejects, so the same `catch` handles it.

