```js
const bcrypt = require("bcrypt");

// Registration
const passwordHash = await bcrypt.hash(plainPassword, 12);
// store passwordHash in the users table (e.g. a password_hash TEXT column)

// Login
const ok = await bcrypt.compare(plainPassword, user.password_hash);
if (!ok) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: "Invalid email or password" });
}
```

`12` is the cost factor: each +1 doubles the time. Aim for roughly 100–300 ms per hash on your server and measure it there. bcrypt generates and embeds the salt in the hash string (`$2b$12$...`), so you don't store a separate salt, and you must compare with `bcrypt.compare`, never by hashing again and comparing strings.

Two details: use the async functions, since the sync versions block the event loop for the whole hash time; and bcrypt only uses the first 72 bytes of the password, so cap the length in validation (for example 64 characters) rather than letting longer passwords silently collide on their prefix.

I ran this with `bcrypt` 6: `compare` returns `true` for the right password and `false` for a wrong one. If the native build of `bcrypt` gives you trouble on your platform, `bcryptjs` is a pure-JS package with the same API, just slower. Argon2id (`argon2` package) is the newer recommendation and a fine choice for a new project.

