Roll it out in report-only mode first, then enforce. A React build compiles JSX to external JS files, so the hard part is usually inline styles and any third-party scripts.

Starting policy for a typical SPA that talks to an API:

```
Content-Security-Policy:
  default-src 'self';
  script-src 'self';
  connect-src 'self' https://api.example;
  img-src 'self' data:;
  style-src 'self';
  object-src 'none';
  base-uri 'self';
  frame-ancestors 'none'
```

Practical notes:

- `connect-src` must list every origin your `fetch`/XHR/WebSocket talks to, or API calls get blocked.
- Inline styles: some libraries inject them. Prefer moving to CSS files; if you can't, you'll need a nonce/hash or, as a last resort, `'unsafe-inline'` in `style-src` only (never in `script-src`).
- Avoid `'unsafe-inline'`/`'unsafe-eval'` in `script-src`. If a dependency needs `eval`, that's worth reconsidering.
- Set it as a real response header from your server/CDN, not just a `<meta>` tag (a meta CSP can't set `frame-ancestors`).

Deploy with `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` plus a `report-uri`/`report-to` endpoint, watch what it would block for a few days, tighten, then switch to the enforcing header.

