rust beginner here, what does this mean

```
error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: `names`
```

```rust
let names = vec![String::from("Ana"), String::from("Luis")];
for n in names {
    println!("{}", n);
}
println!("{}", names.len());
```

The `for` loop takes ownership of `names`. `for n in names` consumes the vector and hands you each `String` by value, so after the loop `names` no longer exists and `names.len()` can't borrow it.

Loop over a reference instead, which only borrows the vector:

```rust
for n in &names {
    println!("{}", n);
}
println!("{}", names.len());
```

Inside the loop `n` is now a `&String`, which `println!` prints the same way. Use `for n in names` only when you don't need the vector after the loop.

quick one, anything wrong here?
```php
<?php
$page = $_GET['page'] ?? 'home';
include "pages/" . $page . ".php";
```

Yes, it is a local file inclusion. The visitor controls the path passed to `include`, and PHP executes whatever file it includes.

By putting `../` segments in `page`, an attacker can make the app run any `.php` file on the server outside `pages/`, such as an admin script that assumes it is never reached directly. The worst case is combining it with any upload feature that lets someone store a file ending in `.php`: including that file runs their code on your server.

Use an allowlist instead of building a path from input:

```php
<?php
$pages = ['home', 'about', 'contact'];
$page = $_GET['page'] ?? 'home';
if (!in_array($page, $pages, true)) {
    http_response_code(404);
    $page = 'home';
}
include __DIR__ . "/pages/{$page}.php";
```

The strict `in_array(..., true)` avoids type-juggling surprises, and `__DIR__` makes the path independent of the current working directory.

The `.json()` call binds tighter than `await`, so it runs on the Promise that `fetch` returns, not on the Response, and a Promise has no `.json` method. Await the response first, then read the body:

```js
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/users");
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const data = await res.json();
```

The `res.ok` check is worth keeping: `fetch` only rejects on network failures, so a 404 or 500 would otherwise reach `res.json()` and fail with a confusing parse error.

