Types don't exist at runtime, so the only way to be sure is to validate the data. For a small shape, a hand-written type guard is enough:

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

function isUser(x: unknown): x is User {
  return (
    typeof x === "object" && x !== null &&
    "id" in x && typeof x.id === "number" &&
    "name" in x && typeof x.name === "string"
  );
}

if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const data: unknown = await res.json();
if (!isUser(data)) throw new Error("Unexpected response shape");
// data is User from here on
```

For bigger or nested shapes a schema library saves a lot of code and gives you the type for free. With zod:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const UserSchema = z.object({ id: z.number(), name: z.string() });
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;

const user = UserSchema.parse(await res.json()); // throws if it doesn't match
```

Note the `res.ok` check: `fetch` does not reject on 404 or 500, only on network errors.

Grid and flex columns don't flow from one column into the next: each column is a separate box, and when printed it just breaks across pages on its own. Text that should read like a newspaper (down the first column, then the second, then onto the next page) needs CSS multi-column layout:

```css
@media print {
  .article-body {
    display: block;        /* undo the screen grid */
    columns: 2;
    column-gap: 8mm;
    hyphens: auto;
  }
  .article-body h2,
  .article-body figure.wide {
    column-span: all;      /* full-width across both columns */
  }
  .article-body h2 {
    break-after: avoid;
  }
  .article-body figure {
    break-inside: avoid;
  }
  .article-body p {
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
  }
}
```

The browser now fills column one, then column two, then continues on the next page. This means the content must be one continuous flow in the HTML, not split into two wrapper divs. If your markup currently has `<div class="col-left">` and `<div class="col-right">`, the print version needs them to be a single sequence of paragraphs.

`hyphens: auto` only works if the page declares its language (`<html lang="en">`), and it helps a lot in narrow columns, where long words otherwise leave big gaps. Keep figures that must stay whole shorter than a page, or `break-inside: avoid` can't help.

