Reviewing a PR, this greeting endpoint looks off to me but I can't say why:

```python
@app.route("/hello")
def hello():
    name = request.args.get("name", "friend")
    template = "<h1>Hello " + name + "!</h1>"
    return render_template_string(template)
```

Your instinct is right: this is server-side template injection, and in Flask that can lead to remote code execution.

The user's input becomes part of the template source, so Jinja2 evaluates any template expression inside `name` instead of printing it. Template expressions can reach Python objects, and public research shows how to get from there to running commands on the server, so treat it as critical.

Keep the template constant and pass the name as a variable:

```python
@app.route("/hello")
def hello():
    name = request.args.get("name", "friend")
    return render_template_string("<h1>Hello {{ name }}!</h1>", name=name)
```

Now `name` is data, and Jinja's autoescaping also neutralizes HTML in it, which covers the XSS this endpoint would otherwise have too. Worth searching the codebase for other `render_template_string` calls built by concatenation.

