`flex-grow: 1` splits the *leftover* space equally, but each column starts from its content width (`flex-basis: auto`), so the one with more text starts wider and stays wider. Use `flex: 1`, which sets the basis to 0 so the whole width is shared equally, and add `min-width: 0` so a long word or a wide element can't force one column open:

```css
.plans {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

.plan {
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 0;
}
```

Grid is often simpler for this, since equal columns are what it does by default:

```css
.plans {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

The `minmax(0, 1fr)` matters for the same reason as `min-width: 0`: a plain `1fr` won't shrink below the content's minimum width.

On a phone three columns won't fit, so start with a single column and switch to three from a breakpoint:

```css
.plans {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

@media (min-width: 48rem) {
  .plans {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
}
```

