Set one CSS custom property from JS and let CSS do the rest:

```js
function setProgress(bar, pct) {
  bar.style.setProperty('--progress', pct);
  bar.toggleAttribute('data-high', pct >= 80);
  bar.setAttribute('aria-valuenow', pct);
}
```

```html
<div class="progress" role="progressbar" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="0" aria-label="Upload"></div>
```

```css
.progress {
  --progress: 0;
  height: 0.5rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: var(--color-track);
}

.progress::before {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  height: 100%;
  width: calc(var(--progress) * 1%);
  border-radius: inherit;
  background: var(--color-accent);
  transition: width 0.2s;
}

.progress[data-high]::before {
  background: var(--color-warning);
}
```

JS now passes only data (a number and a flag), and every visual decision lives in the stylesheet. The value is unitless and `calc(var(--progress) * 1%)` adds the unit, so the same number also works in `aria-valuenow`.

To read a custom property back in JS, use `getComputedStyle(bar).getPropertyValue('--progress')`; note that it returns a string.

If you don't need custom styling, the native `<progress value="40" max="100">` element is accessible out of the box and only needs `progress.value = pct`.

```js
app.get('/cerca', async (req, res) => {
  const q = req.query.q || '';
  const risultati = await Articolo.cerca(q);
  if (risultati.length === 0) {
