`display` can't be transitioned: the element switches from `none` to `block` in one step, and on the frame it appears there's no "previous" opacity to start the fade from. Hide it with `visibility` instead, which works together with the opacity transition:

```css
.dropdown {
  opacity: 0;
  visibility: hidden;
  transition: opacity 0.2s, visibility 0.2s;
}

.dropdown.open {
  opacity: 1;
  visibility: visible;
}
```

`visibility` switches in a single step too, but the browser keeps it `visible` for the whole duration of a transition that goes to or from `visible`, so the fade-out plays fully before it becomes hidden. Unlike `opacity: 0` on its own, `visibility: hidden` also removes the menu links from the Tab order and from screen readers, so keyboard users don't land on invisible items.

The element still takes up space in the layout, but a dropdown is normally `position: absolute`, so that doesn't matter here.

