Serve `dist` as static files, and add a fallback that returns `index.html` for any other GET so the client-side router can handle paths like `/dashboard`. Order matters: API routes first, then static files, then the fallback.

```js
const path = require("node:path");
const express = require("express");

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

app.use("/api", require("./routes/api"));

const dist = path.join(__dirname, "../client/dist");
app.use(express.static(dist));

// Express 5 syntax; on Express 4 the path is "*"
app.get("/{*splat}", (req, res) => {
  res.sendFile(path.join(dist, "index.html"));
});
```

The refresh fails today because the browser asks the server for `/dashboard`, and only React Router knows that page; the fallback hands the browser the app, which then renders the right route.

One detail: an unknown `/api/whatever` would also fall through to `index.html` and return HTML with a 200. Add an `/api` 404 handler before the static middleware so API clients get a proper JSON 404:

```js
app.use("/api", (req, res) => res.status(404).json({ error: "Not found" }));
```

I checked the `/{*splat}` pattern on Express 5.2: it matches `/` as well as nested paths, while a bare `*` throws at startup.

Don't: anyone who can guess or read a user ID can build that token, so it resets any account. The token must be random, single-use, short-lived, and stored only as a hash.

```js
const crypto = require("node:crypto");

// POST /forgot-password
const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString("hex");
const tokenHash = crypto.createHash("sha256").update(token).digest("hex");
await db.query(
  `INSERT INTO password_resets (user_id, token_hash, expires_at)
   VALUES ($1, $2, now() + interval '1 hour')`,
  [user.id, tokenHash]
);
// email a link: https://app.example.com/reset?token=<token>
```

When the link is used, hash the received token, look up a row with that hash and `expires_at > now()`, set the new password (bcrypt), then delete that row, plus any other reset tokens for the user.

A fast hash like SHA-256 is fine here, unlike for passwords, because the token has 256 bits of randomness and can't be guessed. Storing only the hash means a leaked database doesn't hand out working reset links.

Also: reply "if that address exists, we've sent an email" whether or not the account exists, and invalidate the user's existing sessions after a successful reset.

