```js
app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => callback(null, true),
  credentials: true,
}));
```

No: it allows every website to make authenticated requests to your API and read the responses.

The callback accepts any origin, and with `credentials: true` the `cors` package reflects the requesting origin back in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and adds `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. The concrete attack: a logged-in user visits `evil.example.net`, whose script calls `fetch("https://api.example.com/me", { credentials: "include" })`. The browser sends the session cookie, and because your headers allow that origin, the script can read the response: profile data, orders, anything the API returns for that user. It can also call state-changing endpoints.

Whether the browser sends the cookie depends on its `SameSite` attribute. Since your frontend and API are on different subdomains of the same site, you may have set `SameSite=None`, in which case the attack works as described. Don't rely on the cookie settings to cover this anyway.

Allow only your own frontend:

```js
const allowedOrigins = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    callback(null, !origin || allowedOrigins.has(origin));
  },
  credentials: true,
}));
```

Requests with no `Origin` header, such as curl or server-to-server calls, are not affected by CORS in the first place, so letting them through here changes nothing about security. Put the dev origin, like `http://localhost:5173`, in the set only when `NODE_ENV` is not production.

```js
const byStatus = Object.groupBy(orders, (order) => order.status);
// { paid: [...], pending: [...] }
```

Your runtime is older than that feature: `Object.groupBy` is recent (Node 21 and current browsers), and I should have said so. `reduce` works everywhere:

```js
const byStatus = orders.reduce((groups, order) => {
  (groups[order.status] ??= []).push(order);
  return groups;
}, {});
```

The `??=` operator needs Node 15 or newer; on anything older, write `groups[order.status] = groups[order.status] || [];` on its own line before the `push`.

