Arrow functions don't get their own `this`; they use the `this` of the code around them. With a regular function, the browser calls your listener with `this` set to the element. With an arrow function that binding is ignored, and `this` is whatever it was outside, which is `undefined` in a module or `window` in a classic script, and `window.classList` doesn't exist.

With arrow functions, use the event object instead:

```js
btn.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  event.currentTarget.classList.toggle('active');
});
```

Use `currentTarget` rather than `target`: `currentTarget` is always the element the listener is attached to, while `target` is the innermost element that was actually clicked, for example an `<svg>` icon inside the button.

In this case you could also just use the variable you already have, `btn.classList.toggle('active')`. Both regular and arrow functions are fine here; the arrow version with `currentTarget` has the advantage that it reads the same way when you later move the handler into a class or a delegated listener.

