feedback on this card css? it's for a feature grid on our landing page

```css
.feature-card {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);
  border-left: 4px solid #f093fb;
  border-radius: 16px;
  padding: 32px;
  text-align: center;
  color: white;
  box-shadow: 0 20px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
}
.feature-card h3 { font-size: 24px; }
.feature-card p { font-size: 14px; opacity: 0.8; }
```

The most urgent problem is readability: the 14px paragraph is white at 80% opacity, which on the light end of the gradient (#667eea) gives a contrast of about 2.9:1. Body text needs at least 4.5:1, so part of every card fails.

Beyond that, this is the most recognizable "generated landing page" look: the #667eea to #764ba2 gradient is a very common default, and a rounded card with a colored left stripe, a heavy shadow and centered text is the same pattern. When every feature is a loud purple card, none of them stands out.

What I would change, in order:

1. Drop the gradient and the stripe. Let the cards sit on the page background and separate them with spacing, not with boxes.
2. Left-align the text and cap the paragraph width so it wraps nicely.
3. Use full-opacity color tokens for text instead of `opacity`, so contrast is predictable.
4. Keep the brand color for the one thing you want clicked (the CTA), not for decoration.

```css
.features {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
  gap: 40px 32px;
}
.feature-card {
  text-align: left;
}
.feature-card h3 {
  font-size: 1.25rem;
  line-height: 1.25;
  margin: 0 0 8px;
  color: var(--text);
}
.feature-card p {
  font-size: 1rem;
  line-height: 1.55;
  margin: 0;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  max-width: 45ch;
}
```

`--text` and `--text-muted` should be your existing color tokens; if you don't have them yet, a near-black and a mid gray tinted slightly toward your brand hue work well, as long as the muted one still reaches 4.5:1 on the background. If the section then feels too plain, add one small consistent icon above each title rather than bringing the box back.

