`res.status(400).json(...)` sends the response but doesn't stop the function. Execution continues, reaches `res.json(user)`, and tries to send a second response on the same request. Return after responding:

```js
if (!req.body.email) {
  return res.status(400).json({ error: "email required" });
}
```

The same bug shows up whenever two branches can both reach a `res.*` call, for example calling `res.json()` inside a loop or both in a `.then` and after it. When you see this error, look for the path that responds twice.

Yes, the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query. Users turn on "reduce motion" in their OS settings (macOS, Windows, iOS and Android all have it) and the browser exposes it to CSS and JS.

In CSS, wrap the big movements or turn them off for those users:

```css
.card {
  animation: slide-in 0.6s ease-out both;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .card {
    animation: fade-in 0.2s ease-out both;
  }
}
```

Replacing a large movement with a short fade is usually better than removing all feedback. Parallax and big slides are what cause trouble; opacity changes and small color transitions are generally fine.

For motion driven by JS (parallax on scroll, auto-playing carousels), check the same preference:

```js
const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

if (!reduceMotion.matches) {
  startParallax();
}
```

Also check `scroll-behavior: smooth` if you use it, since long smooth scrolls bother the same people:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
}
```

To test, turn on the setting in your OS (on macOS: Accessibility, Display, Reduce motion) or emulate it in Chrome DevTools under Rendering, "Emulate CSS media feature prefers-reduced-motion".

